From fc7263a92007ca6e3221404ab48165c6c04eed6d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 13:39:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] css: implement contrast-color() and style() container queries
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WPT issue #1491 problem 6, css-color/contrast-color-style-query.html, needs
three features to compose: an @property-registered <color> custom property,
contrast-color(#000) resolving to an absolute colour, and
@container style(--contrast-color: white) matching on it. Registration
already worked; the other two did not, so the test rendered 100% white
against Chromium's green.

contrast-color(<color>) resolves to whichever of black or white contrasts
more with its argument. The choice uses the WCAG 2 contrast ratio over
relative luminance, and collapses to a single threshold: white wins exactly
below luminance sqrt(1.05 * 0.05) - 0.05, where the two ratios are equal.
That is NOT mid-grey — #767676 takes black while #757575 takes white. Wired
into CssValueParser.TryParseColor, so it is a <color> everywhere; system
colours are routed there in the same pass, since they are <color> keywords
too.

style() container queries were explicitly unsupported and forced the whole
query false. They now evaluate against a style container, which — unlike a
size container — needs no container-type: css-contain-3 makes every element
one, so size and style containers are resolved separately and a style-only
query still works where no size container exists.

Two parsing traps this uncovered, both of which silently made every style
query false rather than erroring:

  - SplitContainerName read the leading identifier of "style(...)" as a
    CONTAINER NAME, so the lookup hunted for container-name: style, found
    nothing, and bailed. An identifier immediately followed by "(" is a
    function, not a name.
  - The condition tokenizer split "style" from its parenthesised argument,
    leaving the argument looking like a nested condition and the name like a
    bare size feature. Function tokens now keep their argument list.

Comparison is colour-aware: a registered <color> property computes to an
absolute colour, so "white" and "rgb(255, 255, 255)" are the same computed
value and must compare equal.

Known gaps, deliberate: only the custom-property form of style() is
supported (a standard-property query returns false rather than guessing),
and a registration with inherits: false is not honoured by the ancestor
walk. The walk uses cascaded declarations rather than GetComputedStyle
because this runs during style computation and re-entering it for an
ancestor would recurse.
